            <ArticleTitle>[Hypoalphacholesterolemia and coronary disease (an approach using a factor analysis)].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The data obtained from a screening of 1,467 males aged 40-59 years made it possible to characterize the relation of dyslipoproteinemias, primarily hypoalphacholesterolemia, to the incidence of coronary heart disease and risk factors in males, by using factorial and multivariate analyses. The findings suggest that hypoalphacholesterolemia is closely associated with hypertriglyceridemia in its atherogenicity, thus from the clinical point of view low levels of HDL cholesterol should be used as a ground for thorough examination of lipid metabolism, with great emphasis on metabolic disturbances of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
